WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.810 --> 00:00:05.080
One of the great things about creating a sprawling
cinematic universe with dozens of characters

00:00:05.080 --> 00:00:08.820
is that the inevitable storyline mistakes
made along the way don't have to be permanent.

00:00:11.060 --> 00:00:11.820
"I'm listening."

00:00:12.200 --> 00:00:16.359
Just like in the comics that came before them,
each new chapter in the Marvel Cinematic Universe

00:00:16.360 --> 00:00:21.500
is an opportunity to reach back into the past,
clarify plot lines, and close plot holes.

00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:25.840
And that's just what the minds behind Avengers:
Infinity War say they did on the revealing

00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:29.160
commentary track for the epic nineteenth movie
in the MCU.

00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:31.860
Warning: massive spoilers ahead!

00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:36.140
Directors Joe and Anthony Russo sat down with
co-writers Christopher Markus and Stephen

00:00:36.150 --> 00:00:40.190
McFeely to break down how they wrangled ten
years' worth of buildup into one cohesive

00:00:40.190 --> 00:00:40.820
climax.

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:45.500
There are a ton of great insights into lingering
questions about the two-and-a-half-hour blockbuster,

00:00:45.500 --> 00:00:49.360
including what has long been considered a
plot hole in the very first Avengers flick

00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:52.380
from 2012, written and directed by Joss Whedon.

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:57.100
You remember the scene: Natasha Romanoff,
Tony Stark and Steve Rogers have Loki in custody

00:00:57.100 --> 00:01:01.560
on the Avengers' Quinjet, when all of a sudden,
Thor crashes down like lightning, knocks Stark

00:01:01.560 --> 00:01:03.460
to the floor, grabs his bro, and bails.

00:01:04.040 --> 00:01:07.920
But how did Thor actually get to Earth from
Asgard, when he destroyed his typical mode

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:11.740
of space travel, the rainbow-colored Bifrost
Bridge, at the end of the first Thor movie?

00:01:11.980 --> 00:01:16.159
Remember: Avengers takes place before the
events of Thor: The Dark World, so the bridge

00:01:16.160 --> 00:01:17.280
had yet to be rebuilt.

00:01:17.509 --> 00:01:21.400
Thor doesn't explain how he made it to Earth
in the first Avengers outing, but Loki assumes

00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:23.260
it has something to do with "dark energy":

00:01:23.720 --> 00:01:28.200
"With the Bifrost gone how much dark energy
did the Allfather have to muster to conjure

00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:28.700
you here?

00:01:29.020 --> 00:01:30.200
Your precious Earth."

00:01:30.580 --> 00:01:35.460
In the Infinity War commentary track, co-writer
Christopher Markus says crediting "dark energy"

00:01:35.460 --> 00:01:40.850
for the trip always seemed like, quote, "a
bit of a paste job", sorry, Joss!, so they

00:01:40.850 --> 00:01:43.360
added a callback in Infinity War to bolster
it.

00:01:43.689 --> 00:01:47.940
The comment comes during an early Infinity
War scene when the Hulk, beaten badly by Thanos

00:01:47.950 --> 00:01:52.250
in one-on-one combat, gets magically beamed
back to the Earth by the Asgardian Heimdall,

00:01:52.250 --> 00:01:54.100
the former guardian of the Bifrost.

00:01:54.420 --> 00:01:58.639
Heimdall's use of "dark magic," which comes
right before his ultimate sacrifice, allows

00:01:58.639 --> 00:02:02.750
the Avengers to have some crucial time to
prepare themselves before Thanos' arrival,

00:02:02.750 --> 00:02:06.540
which makes all the difference in their ability
to resist his invasion as much as they do.

00:02:06.820 --> 00:02:10.840
Addressing unnamed critics who called the
whole magical "dark energy" explanation from

00:02:10.849 --> 00:02:14.860
the first Avengers movie a "cheat," Markus
says Heimdall's summoning of "dark magic"

00:02:14.860 --> 00:02:19.080
in Infinity War can now be seen as clear evidence
that Loki's assumption was correct:

00:02:19.360 --> 00:02:22.300
"You just saw where the dark magic came from:
straight out of Heimdall."

00:02:22.700 --> 00:02:26.960
Thankfully, this vague "dark energy" travel
is no longer necessary for Thor, who is more

00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:30.260
than capable of bridging the cold expanse
of space on his own by the end of Infinity

00:02:30.260 --> 00:02:30.760
War.

00:02:30.980 --> 00:02:35.240
His new Bifrost-summoning axe Stormbreaker
is his new ride, bringing him to Wakanda for

00:02:35.240 --> 00:02:36.680
the film's devastating climax.

00:02:37.140 --> 00:02:42.220
And he'll need it for what's ahead in the
still untitled Avengers 4, due out May 2019,

00:02:42.230 --> 00:02:46.599
which rumor has it will feature at least one
time-travel or flashback scene straight out

00:02:46.600 --> 00:02:48.040
of the original Avengers flick.

00:02:48.460 --> 00:02:51.960
So expect your understanding of the events
of that film to shift yet again.

00:02:52.180 --> 00:02:57.240
Avengers: Infinity War will be out on 4K Blu-Ray,
standard Blu-Ray, and DVD on August 14th.

00:02:57.400 --> 00:03:01.140
If you're itching to hear the full commentary
track, it's available as a digital download

00:03:01.150 --> 00:03:02.200
from select outlets.

